(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-neck-masses-neoplasms-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Neck Masses and Neoplasms Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which physical finding characterises a lymphangioma?","choices":["Pulsatility with an audible bruit","Vertical movement with tongue protrusion","Enlargement on crying or straining","Positive transillumination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A lymphangioma is a congenital malformation in which lymph spaces fail to connect to the rest of the lymphatic system, so it fills with clear lymph. Clear fluid transmits light, which is why the mass transilluminates, and it also explains the soft, doughy, compressible texture. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which congenital neck mass presents as a midline, non-tender, mobile submental mass that does NOT move with tongue protrusion?","choices":["Thymic cyst","Dermoid cyst","Thyroglossal duct cyst","Branchial cleft cyst"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A dermoid arises from epithelium entrapped in deeper tissue during embryogenesis or by traumatic implantation. It shares a midline submental position with the thyroglossal duct cyst, which is why they are the pair that must be separated, but it has no attachment to the hyoid and therefore does not rise with the tongue. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A neck mass that is pulsatile or has a bruit indicates what?","choices":["A benign lesion present for years","Lymphoma","A vascular lesion","A cystic lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is one of four rules of thumb. Pulsatility or a bruit means vascular, which puts paraganglioma at the head of the differential and makes computed tomography angiography the next investigation. It also means the mass must not be biopsied blindly. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why is incision and drainage avoided in an infected branchial cleft cyst?","choices":["It spreads the infection to the mediastinum","It makes the definitive excision harder","It causes the cyst to become malignant","It always leads to a permanent fistula"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Opening the cyst scars the tissue planes, which makes it much more difficult to define and follow the tract at the definitive operation. Since recurrence depends on removing the whole tract, anything that obscures it matters. Even with frank abscess, needle aspiration and decompression are preferred. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is primary thyroid lymphoma treated?","choices":["Total thyroidectomy","Radioiodine ablation","Observation with serial ultrasound","Chemotherapy and radiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike the thyroid carcinomas, which are surgical diseases with radioiodine ablation where indicated, lymphoma is treated systemically. That is the practical consequence of getting the diagnosis right, and it is why an inconclusive aspirate in a rapidly enlarging Hashimoto gland is pursued rather than accepted. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which triangle contains the carotid arteries, internal jugular vein and vagus nerve?","choices":["The submental triangle","The occipital triangle","The carotid triangle","The muscular triangle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The carotid triangle is bounded by sternocleidomastoid behind, the posterior belly of digastric above and omohyoid below, and it houses the carotid arteries, internal jugular vein and vagus nerve. Knowing the boundaries lets you predict what lies beneath before making an incision. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does a HOT nodule on radionuclide scanning imply?","choices":["A low risk of malignancy, so no biopsy is needed","A high risk of malignancy requiring urgent surgery","That the patient is hypothyroid","That contrast imaging is now required"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hot nodule concentrates tracer because it is producing hormone autonomously, and autonomously functioning nodules are almost always benign. That is the entire purpose of scanning before aspirating in a hyperthyroid patient: it identifies the group in whom biopsy can safely be omitted. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the natural history of a head and neck haemangioma?","choices":["Present at birth at full size, then static","Spontaneous resolution within the first month","Rapid growth in the first year, then involution from 18 to 24 months","Steady growth throughout childhood with no involution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Haemangiomas appear in the first few months of life, grow rapidly through the first year, and then begin to involute at 18 to 24 months. About 90 per cent resolve without therapy, which is why observation is the default and why parents need the growth-then-shrink curve explained in advance. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 27"},{"q":"How is toxoplasmosis contracted?","choices":["Unpasteurised milk","Tick bites and contact with rabbits","Poorly cooked meat or ingestion of oocytes in cat faeces","A cat scratch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Toxoplasma gondii reaches humans through undercooked meat or through oocysts shed in cat faeces. Patients present with fever, malaise, sore throat and myalgias alongside the adenopathy, diagnosis is serologic, and treatment is sulfonamides or pyrimethamine. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which muscle divides the posterior triangle into its two subdivisions?","choices":["Omohyoid","Digastric","Sternocleidomastoid","Mylohyoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The omohyoid crosses the posterior triangle and splits it into the occipital triangle above and the supraclavicular triangle below. That division matters because the contents differ sharply: the spinal accessory nerve crosses the occipital floor, while the supraclavicular triangle holds the subclavian artery and brachial plexus. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is first-line drug treatment when a haemangioma requires intervention?","choices":["Propranolol","Systemic corticosteroids","Sclerotherapy","Surgical excision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intervention is reserved for airway compromise, skin ulceration, dysphagia, thrombocytopenia or cardiac failure, since most lesions resolve untreated. When treatment is needed, propranolol is first line for those without a contraindication, with systemic corticosteroids, interferon alpha or laser excision as second line. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which laboratory tests are listed in the workup of a persistent neck mass?","choices":["Full blood count, HIV and Epstein-Barr serology, inflammatory markers and thyroid panels","Clotting studies alone","Arterial blood gases","Serum calcitonin in every patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The panel is broad because the differential is broad: blood count and metabolic profile, HIV and Epstein-Barr and cytomegalovirus serology, erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein, an autoimmune panel, thyroid and parathyroid hormones, and tuberculin skin testing with cat scratch and toxoplasmosis titres. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which thyroid carcinoma spreads by blood to bone and lung?","choices":["Follicular carcinoma","Papillary carcinoma","Anaplastic carcinoma","Primary thyroid lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The route of spread is what distinguishes follicular from papillary disease. Papillary tends to involve local cervical nodes, whereas follicular disseminates haematogenously to bone and lungs. A distant bony deposit in a thyroid cancer therefore points to the follicular type. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"How many separate needle passes are required for an adequate fine needle aspiration?","choices":["A minimum of four","One, provided material is obtained","Two","Ten"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sampling error is the main limitation of aspiration cytology, because a node may contain a small focus of tumour among otherwise reactive tissue. Four separate passes sample different parts of the node and make a falsely reassuring result much less likely. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which operation is specified for anaplastic thyroid carcinoma?","choices":["Total thyroidectomy with neck dissection","Lobectomy alone","No surgery at all","Isthmectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Almost all thyroid cancers require thyroidectomy, with two exceptions. One is disease localised to a single lobe that is well differentiated with no metastasis; the other is anaplastic carcinoma, where isthmectomy is what is specified, because the disease is not curable by resection and the operation serves a different purpose. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 44"},{"q":"Into what do the deep cervical nodes on the LEFT side ultimately drain?","choices":["The cisterna chyli","The thoracic duct","The right lymphatic duct","Directly into the superior vena cava"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The deep cervical chain empties into the thoracic duct on the left, which itself joins the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. On the right the equivalent structure is the right lymphatic duct, draining into the corresponding location on that side. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which benign neck tumour is a vascular neoplasm arising at the carotid body?","choices":["Schwannoma","Lipoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Paraganglioma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Paragangliomas head the benign vascular list, arising at the carotid body, the vagus or the jugulotympanic region. Their vascularity is what produces a pulsatile mass with a bruit, and it is also why the mass must be imaged with angiography rather than biopsied blindly. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why is fine needle aspiration preferred to excisional biopsy for a suspected malignant neck node?","choices":["Aspiration allows immunohistochemistry that excision does not","Excision spills tumour and complicates definitive treatment","Aspiration is more accurate than excision","Excision requires a general anaesthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Opening a malignant node breaches its capsule and releases cells into the surrounding tissue planes, seeding the neck beyond the original nodal compartment. That makes both the surgical field and the radiotherapy field harder to define afterwards, whereas aspiration samples the node without opening it. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause suppurative cervical lymphadenopathy?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus and group A beta-haemolytic Streptococcus","Bartonella henselae and Toxoplasma gondii","Francisella tularensis and Brucella","Candida and Aspergillus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These two account for most suppurative adenitis, producing masses in the submandibular or jugulodigastric regions with sore throat, skin lesions and upper respiratory symptoms. Treatment is empirical cover against anaerobes and gram-positive organisms, with aspiration or drainage if antibiotics fail. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 33"},{"q":"Most neck masses in patients under 40 are of what nature?","choices":["Congenital, with infection as the exception","Vascular, with neoplasia as the exception","Inflammatory, with Hodgkin lymphoma as the exception","Neoplastic, with infection as the exception"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Under 40 the great majority of neck masses are inflammatory, usually reactive adenopathy following an upper respiratory infection. Hodgkin lymphoma is named as the specific exception, which is why a persistent painless node in a young patient still deserves attention despite the favourable base rate. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is a laryngocele?","choices":["A mucous retention cyst of the floor of the mouth","An abnormal dilation or herniation of the laryngeal saccule","A keratin-filled sac behind the tympanic membrane","A congenital malformation of lymphatic channels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The saccule is a small outpouching of the laryngeal ventricle, and sustained raised intraluminal pressure can dilate or herniate it. Symptoms are cough, hoarseness, dyspnoea, dysphagia or a foreign body sensation, and secondary infection of one is called a laryngopyocele. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the natural history of a sternocleidomastoid tumour of infancy?","choices":["Resolves within two weeks without intervention","Transforms into a rhabdomyosarcoma if untreated","Enlarges for 2 to 3 months, then regresses over 4 to 8","Grows continuously and always requires excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mass grows for a couple of months and then shrinks over the following four to eight, with about 80 per cent resolving spontaneously. Physical therapy is given during that time to prevent a fixed restrictive torticollis, and surgery is reserved for the minority that persist. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"Where does a branchial cleft cyst characteristically present?","choices":["In the midline of the anterior neck","In the posterior triangle","Within the sternocleidomastoid muscle itself","Laterally, at the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Branchial cleft cysts arise from pharyngobranchial ducts that fail to obliterate during fetal development, and they sit laterally along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. That position is the first branch of the congenital differential: lateral against midline. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does laryngoscopy show in a laryngocele?","choices":["Bilateral symmetric whitish nodules at the anterior third","A smooth dilation at the level of the false cord","Multiple friable warty growths on both cords","A unilateral pedunculated mass on the vibrating margin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The saccule lies between the true and false cords, so its dilation bulges at the false cord and appears smooth because it is lined by normal mucosa. Computed tomography then confirms the diagnosis and defines the extent, which determines whether decompression or open excision is used. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 24"},{"q":"A cervical node in a child warrants investigation if it persists beyond what interval?","choices":["Six months","Three days","Four to six weeks","One week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reactive nodes regress in one to two weeks, so persistence well beyond that is what changes the picture. The threshold is four to six weeks, or any node that continues to enlarge, and biopsy then looks for fungal, granulomatous or neoplastic processes. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why is iodine-containing contrast avoided when thyroid cancer is suspected?","choices":["It triggers a thyroid storm in all patients","It compromises subsequent radioiodine treatment","It causes an allergic reaction in thyroid disease","It obscures the thyroid on imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The thyroid takes up iodine avidly, so a contrast load saturates the gland and any tumour within it. Radioiodine therapy depends on that same uptake mechanism, so giving contrast first can delay definitive treatment for weeks until the iodine has cleared. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause fungal neck infection in the immunocompromised?","choices":["Bartonella henselae and Toxoplasma gondii","Candida, Histoplasma and Aspergillus","Staphylococcus aureus and group A Streptococcus","Mycobacterium tuberculosis and atypical mycobacteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immunocompromised patients are particularly susceptible to these three, and the emphasis is that fungal cultures AND serology are needed for diagnosis, because they will not be found unless specifically sought. Treatment is amphotericin B, given aggressively. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 35"},{"q":"From which cells does medullary thyroid carcinoma arise?","choices":["Parafollicular C cells","Thyroid follicular epithelial cells","Lymphocytes within the gland","Parathyroid chief cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. C cells produce calcitonin, which is why calcitonin serves as a tumour marker for detecting residual or recurrent disease. Their neuroendocrine origin also explains the familial association with multiple endocrine neoplasia, which is why family members are screened. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which size threshold appears on the list of features suggesting malignancy in a neck mass?","choices":["Greater than 5 centimetres","Greater than 0.5 centimetres","Greater than 3 centimetres","Greater than 1.5 centimetres"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The malignancy features are: no infectious origin, duration over two weeks, size over 1.5 centimetres, firm and non-cystic and non-tender with little or no mobility, age over 40, and tobacco and alcohol use. Size alone is not diagnostic, but it is one of the criteria that shifts the prior. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"What proportion of congenital neck masses are thyroglossal duct cysts?","choices":["About 5 per cent","About 90 per cent","About 15 per cent","About one third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thyroglossal duct cysts account for roughly a third of all congenital neck masses, which makes them the commonest and gives a midline lump in a child a strong prior probability. Their location can vary, with some presenting lateral or as low as the thyroid gland. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"}]};
